<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="write-a-plugin" title="Write a plugin">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>
            No shipped driver loads a <code>WeaverPlugin</code> of yours. Only a weaver your own
            code builds does — <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a> has
            the boundary.
        </p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        You implement <code>id()</code> and <code>apiLevel()</code>. Register what you add from
        <code>contribute</code>, and hear what the run did in <code>observe</code>. This plugin
        stamps a key into every woven class. It prints each rewrite as well.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="java" src="TracePlugin.java"/>

    <chapter title="The four methods" id="methods">
        <p>
            Two are yours to implement. The other two carry a default. Override the ones you
            need.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="id()">
                <p>
                    Return a constant field. <a href="plugin-lifecycle.topic">The plugin
                    lifecycle</a> says why, and <a href="namespaces.topic">Namespaces and
                    identifiers</a> has the grammar your namespace must satisfy.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="apiLevel()">
                <p>
                    Return <code>WeaverApi.LEVEL</code>, never a literal.
                    <a href="spi.topic">The plugin SPI</a> has the rule and the two codes a
                    mismatch carries.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="contribute(PluginContext)">
                <p>
                    Called once, and everything you add goes through the context it hands you. A
                    throw out of it is <code>AW3115</code>, and the plugin loses what it had
                    already registered. Report through <code>ctx.diagnostics()</code> and return
                    instead — <a href="reporting.topic">Report rather than throw</a>. What may
                    go on the context is in
                    <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="observe(PluginEvent)">
                <p>
                    <code>observeApply()</code> is what buys <code>ClassWoven</code>. Every other
                    event, <code>PluginsLoaded</code>, <code>Prepared</code> and
                    <code>WeavingFinished</code>, reaches every contributor regardless. Keep any
                    state you hold thread-safe: the class loader in the sample below weaves on
                    several threads at once, and <a href="runtime-weaving.topic">Runtime
                    weaving</a> is where that loader is described.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Get it in front of a weaver" id="install">
        <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["Your class implements WeaverPlugin"] --> B{"Which route into the loader?"}
    B -->|plugin| C["An instance handed to the builder"]
    B -->|discoverPlugins| D["ServiceLoader over a class loader you name"]
    C --> E["Load gates admit or drop it"]
    D --> E
    E --> F["contribute runs once"]
    F --> G["PluginsLoaded, then Prepared"]
    G --> H["Weaving, and ClassWoven per class"]
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Both routes belong to whoever builds the weaver. <code>plugin(...)</code> adds rather
            than replaces. The launcher below drives one class through a weaving class loader.
            <a href="runtime-weaving.topic">Runtime weaving</a> covers that loader.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="java" src="Traced.java"/>

        <note>
            <p>
                This plugin records metadata and observes because both reach a weave written with
                annotations. No annotation names a contributed injector kind, and an
                <code>@At</code> naming a contributed point is <code>AW1101</code> wherever the
                processor can check it — name a built-in point there.
                <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a> has both limits.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <procedure title="Compile it and run it" id="run">
        <step>
            <p>Resolve <code>aether-weaver-runtime</code> and <code>aether-weaver-processor</code>,
                with their transitive dependencies, into <path>lib</path>. Neither jar is shaded;
                you need the engine and the API alongside them, which a dependency manager pulls
                in on its own. <a href="artifacts.topic">Artefacts and modules</a> has the
                coordinates and the dependency graph.</p>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Build the fixture into <path>plugins</path>, which nothing else is on. Those two
                classes are <a href="first-weave.topic">Your first weave</a>, and the processor
                writes the manifest that <a href="weave-discovery.topic">discovery</a>
                reads.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
javac -proc:full -cp "lib/*" \
      -d plugins src/fixture/Target.java src/fixture/Audit.java
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Build your plugin and the launcher into <path>classes</path>.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
javac -cp "lib/*" -d classes \
      src/acme/TracePlugin.java src/launcher/Traced.java
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Run it, keeping <path>plugins</path> away from the classpath you pass.</p>
            <code-block lang="bash"><![CDATA[
java -cp "classes:lib/*" launcher.Traced
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Three lines. Your observer prints the first; the other two come off the
                <code>@Woven</code> annotation the weaver left behind.</p>
            <code-block lang="console"><![CDATA[
acme wove fixture/Target: 1 applied
[:%version%, acme:1.0]
[acme:trace=on]
]]></code-block>
            <p><code>acme:1.0</code> beside the engine's own coordinate is proof that the loader
                admitted your plugin. <code>acme:trace=on</code> is your key with your namespace
                applied to it. A stamp carrying neither means the plugin was never admitted; the
                reason is on the line <code>LOG</code> printed. A changed value moves the
                fingerprint — <a href="determinism.topic">Determinism</a> says what that costs.</p>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <chapter title="Ship it for discovery" id="service">
        <p>
            <code>discoverPlugins(loader)</code> asks <code>ServiceLoader</code> for
            <code>WeaverPlugin</code> on the loader you name. Add one provider file there,
            holding the single line <code>acme.TracePlugin</code>.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="text">
classes/META-INF/services/
    de.splatgames.aether.weaver.api.spi.WeaverPlugin
        </code-block>

        <p>
            The same run then loads it without the <code>plugin(...)</code> call, and every gate
            still applies. <a href="loading-plugins.topic">Loading and isolating plugins</a> covers
            the allowlist and a provider that fails to construct.
        </p>
    </chapter>

